**What I ran.** DIRECTIVE task 3, exploration track: do public daily macro series condition the next-day result of gold wall-to-wall reaccel setups? I fetched four FRED series (10y real yield, 10y nominal, broad dollar, VIX) with no API key, joined them to the existing 19404-trade scan cache under a strict availability rule (FRED date D usable only for entries after JST D+1 07:00), and tested 8 pre-registered cells: "prior-day shock in top 20%" and "prior-day direction aligned with trade side" for each series. In-sample 2010-20, out-of-sample 2021-26, recent 2024-26 reported separately per the D2 rule. Runtime 18.6 s, no scan, no M5 load, no MT5 connection.

**Result: null.** 0 of 8 cells pass. The out-of-sample family-wise permutation p is 0.73. The only in-sample cell below nominal 5% (dollar alignment, t −2.41) points the opposite way from the prediction and shrinks out-of-sample. Effect sizes are bounded at roughly ±0.05R everywhere.

**Strongest counter-argument recorded.** The dollar-alignment cell is negative in all three windows, so a reversed hypothesis ("gold wall-to-wall runs worse the day after a same-direction dollar shock") keeps its sign. It was not tested here because the direction was seen after the fact. It is logged as a new backlog item to be tested on post-2026-07 data or other markets. The deeper issue is granularity: gold's rate and dollar sensitivity is intraday co-movement, so the real test needs DXY and US10Y M5-H1 history. That is logged as needs data, tied to the pending MT5 probe.

